.TH PIQUELD 8 "" "piqueld 0.1.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
piqueld \- open\-source deployment for one Linux server
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpiqueld\fR [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Run the piqueld single\-node Docker control plane.
.PP
piqueld deploys applications to the Docker Engine of the host it runs on, as a single\-node Swarm. From each application\*(Aqs manifest, it pulls or builds the images, runs the services, routes HTTPS traffic to them, and keeps them running. You manage it through its dashboard, the \fBpiquelctl\fR CLI, or its HTTP API.
.PP
Without a command, \fBpiqueld\fR runs the daemon until it is stopped. It serves the dashboard and the API on the addresses its configuration\*(Aqs \fBserver.listen_mode\fR selects, and the API on the Unix socket \fBpiquelctl\fR connects to.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Read daemon configuration from this TOML file.
.RS
.PP
Without this flag, \fB/etc/piqueld/config.toml\fR is read if it exists; otherwise built\-in defaults are used.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Also append JSON logs to this file, created with mode \fB0600\fR.
.RS
.PP
Standard output keeps its usual format, so tools can query the logs while a person watches the terminal.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print the options and commands: a summary with \fB\-h\fR, everything with \fB\-\-help\fR.
.TP
\fB\-V, \-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.SH COMMANDS
.PP
A command does its work, then exits. It doesn\*(Aqt run the daemon.
.TP
\fBpiqueld check\-config\fR
Check a configuration file\*(Aqs syntax, types, ranges, and combinations, then exit.
.TP
\fBpiqueld restore\fR
Restore a backup of the data directory into an empty data directory, then exit.
.SS
piqueld check\-config
.PP
Check a configuration file\*(Aqs syntax, types, ranges, and combinations, then exit.
.PP
Nothing on the host is touched: no state, sockets, Docker, or network. Credential files are only located, not read, so the check also runs where they are absent, such as a Nix build.
.PP
\fBpiqueld check\-config\fR \-\-config <PATH>
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
The TOML file to check. Required.
.SS
piqueld restore
.PP
Restore a backup of the data directory into an empty data directory, then exit.
.PP
Run it as the daemon\*(Aqs user while the daemon is stopped. It first checks the backup: the database\*(Aqs integrity, that the database is the one the backup describes, and that \fBsecrets.key\fR decrypts its secrets. It restores nothing if a check fails. The daemon updates the restored database when it next starts.
.PP
\fBpiqueld restore\fR [OPTIONS] <BACKUP>
.TP
\fI<BACKUP>\fR
The backup, such as \fB/var/lib/piqueld/backups/pre\-<schema>\-<time>\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Restore into this data directory instead of the configuration\*(Aqs \fBserver.data_dir\fR. The configuration isn\*(Aqt read then.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Only check the backup, in a temporary directory, and restore nothing.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
\fBSIGTERM, SIGINT\fR
Stop the daemon. It finishes the requests it is serving and stops its work, within a deadline, then exits. A second signal makes it exit at once. The applications it deployed keep running in Docker.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
The daemon stopped after a signal, or the command succeeded.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The daemon or the command failed, and standard error says why. Also when the daemon didn\*(Aqt stop within its deadline, or a second signal stopped it.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The command line is invalid.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB/etc/piqueld/config.toml\fR
The configuration read without \fB\-\-config\fR, in TOML. https://docs.piqueld.dev/user/reference/configuration\-settings.html describes every setting.
.TP
\fB/var/lib/piqueld\fR
The data directory, \fBserver.data_dir\fR, private to the daemon\*(Aqs user: the database \fBpiqueld.db\fR, the key \fBsecrets.key\fR that encrypts the secrets in it, and the rest of the daemon\*(Aqs state. Before the daemon updates the database of an older version, it saves a backup in \fBbackups\fR, which \fBpiqueld restore\fR restores.
.TP
\fB/run/piqueld/piqueld.sock\fR
The Unix socket that serves the API, in \fBserver.runtime_dir\fR. \fBpiquelctl\fR connects to it unless told otherwise.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBpiquelctl\fR(1), \fBdockerd\fR(8), \fBsystemctl\fR(1)
.PP
The documentation, at https://docs.piqueld.dev.
